        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe Luhn logic is in the wrong unit. This codebase already has an established, visible pattern for non-trivial derived values, and the new method breaks from it while sitting right next to two examples of it.\n\nFindings:\n\n1. **Extract the algorithm to a utility class under `rest.function.common`, matching `Localities`/`IdentityKey`.** Two of the mappings immediately adjacent to the new one delegate to dedicated final helper classes via fully-qualified expressions:\n   - `locality` \u2192 `...rest.function.common.Localities.of(...)` (`OwnerMapper.java:26-28`)\n   - `identityKey` \u2192 `...rest.function.owner.IdentityKey.ofOwner(owner)` (`OwnerMapper.java:34-36`)\n\n   A self-contained ~20-line algorithm (digit crunch + Luhn doubling) is exactly the kind of logic those classes exist to hold. It should be a `Luhn` class in `rest.function.common` with a `static int checkDigit(String)`, referenced as `java(...common.Luhn.checkDigit(owner.getCustomerCode()))` \u2014 consistent with `locality` two lines above.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper` is becoming a catch-all.** The existing `default` methods in this interface are either trivial mapping predicates (`hasEmail`, `OwnerMapper.java:45-48`) or MapStruct assembly plumbing (`toOwnerPageDto`, `OwnerMapper.java:82-90`). Neither is a general algorithm. Adding a reusable check-digit routine here mixes domain algorithm code into a mapping interface \u2014 the very concern the `common` package was created to keep out. A maintainer looking for the Luhn routine would expect it beside `Localities`, not buried as a mapper default.\n\nThe OpenAPI addition and the algorithm itself read fine; the only issue is placement/consistency."
      }
